title: Tree names
tags: [[TreeRef forest]]
kind: reference
summary: A node's name is its exact bytes; its text shows valid UTF-8 as is and each undecodable byte as %XX — so a lookup searches both readings.

A POSIX file name is a ''byte string'' — anything but `/` and NUL — and need not be
UTF-8; old collections are full of latin-1 names, and such a file must be tracked
like any other. So a node's name carries its ''exact bytes'', and its text is derived
from them:

* the ''bytes are the identity'': equality, order and sibling uniqueness go through them, so two files whose names differ only in undecodable bytes are two nodes;
* the ''text is presentation'', and what a query spells. A name that is text shows exactly as it is — `100%.txt` stays `100%.txt` — so the common case pays nothing. Only a byte no text can represent becomes `%XX`, its value in hex: `caf\xE9.mp4` shows as `caf%E9.mp4`, one escape per faulty byte.

The split is forced: an encoding that leaves every valid UTF-8 name unchanged
''and'' is injective cannot exist — valid text uses up the whole text form, leaving
nowhere free for an undecodable name. So identity lives in the bytes, and the text is
not a bijection: a file really named `caf%E9.mp4` shows the same text.

Every path the daemon finds on disk carries the bytes end to end: the reconcile walk,
the watcher and its buffered events, the executor. Where text is meant, text is used:
`mf_ignore` patterns are regexes over displayed paths, and responses report the
displayed form. Two gaps remain ([[Path representation]]): a path ''argument''
(`POST /track`, `mf track`) crosses the JSON API as a string, so it must be valid
UTF-8; and the clients that move files (rollback, sync) join the displayed path onto
the root, which misses such a file.

!! Looking a name up

A typed component has up to two byte readings, and ''both are searched, the results
added'':

* ''verbatim'' — the bytes of what was typed: what finds a file whose name really holds those characters. It is the only reading for text that is not the escaped form: no escape, a malformed one (`100%.txt`, `%zz`, `%E`), an escape below `0x80` (such a byte is always valid UTF-8, so never escaped — which keeps `50%2E.txt` from also being searched as `50..txt`), or U+FFFD (a lossy rendering pasted from another tool: the byte it stood for is gone);
* ''escaped'' — the bytes the `%XX` form decodes to, when it is one.

A query returns a set, so returning both is honest. A ''resolution'' that must yield
one node — `tree/resolve-path`, `mf path`, the GUI's navigation — cannot add them: it
takes a `form` (`verbatim` or `escaped`) choosing one reading, and without it two
matches are an ambiguity, answered as no match rather than a guess. Lookups also
follow the repository's [[case sensitivity|Case sensitivity]].

!! On the wire

A name that is valid UTF-8 is a plain JSON string. Only a name no text can represent
takes the object form, with what to show and what identifies it — `bytes` (lowercase
hex) is authoritative:

```
{"type": "tree_ref", "value": {"parent": null, "name": "vidéo.mp4"}}
{"type": "tree_ref", "value": {"parent": null, "name": {"text": "caf%E9.mp4", "bytes": "636166e92e6d7034"}}}
```

A client that assumed a string meets a different shape rather than a silently wrong
name.
